\title{Cyclic Groups and the Discrete Logarithm}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-21}

\p{
    The setting the classical proofs of knowledge are stated over. A cyclic group #{G} of prime order #{q} with generator #{g}: every element of #{G} is #{g^w} for exactly one exponent #{w} in #{\mathbb{Z}_q}, so exponentiation is a bijection from #{\mathbb{Z}_q} to #{G} and exponent arithmetic is arithmetic in a prime field. The discrete logarithm problem is to invert that bijection — given #{h \in G}, find #{w} with #{g^w = h} — and it is assumed hard in the groups used in practice, which are elliptic curve groups over a large prime field.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{Cyclic group, order, generator; why prime order rather than merely cyclic, and what breaks at composite order.}
        \li{Why prime #{q} makes every nonzero exponent difference invertible — the single step [Schnorr](dhsorens-004V) extraction turns on.}
        \li{The discrete logarithm assumption and its relatives, computational and decisional Diffie–Hellman, and which of them a given [sigma protocol](dhsorens-004U) actually needs.}
        \li{What is known about the hardness: generic-group lower bounds, index calculus, and why elliptic curves rather than the multiplicative group of a prime field.}
        \li{Why this setting is absent from the [FRI](dhsorens-0037) family, and what replaces the group structure there — [fields](dhsorens-002K) and [codes](dhsorens-002O).}
        \li{Post-quantum status: Shor breaks all of it, which is the reason the [lattice protocols](dhsorens-0049) exist.}
    }
}

\p{
    Reading: Boneh and Shoup, \em{A Graduate Course in Applied Cryptography}, for the assumptions and their relations; Thaler, \em{Proofs, Arguments, and Zero-Knowledge}, for how they enter the proof systems.
}
